  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1000 AM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nAn upper-level high in eastern Texas and an upper-level trough in\nsouthern California will funnel additional monsoonal moisture into\nour region today, bringing with it more showers and thunderstorms. \nDona Ana County, Otero County, El Paso County, and Hudspeth County \nare favored for thunderstorm development. Storms are expected to \nfire up in the Sacramento Mountains in the early afternoon hours, \nfollowed by a shortwave moving from the southwest from Chihauhua \nin the early evening. These regions are still under a Flood Watch \nthrough late tonight, and up to an additional inch of rain on \nalready saturated ground could lead to flooding conditions. The \nwestern edge of this wave will need to be closely examined as the \nnight progresses, as the most recent HRRR runs have a band of \nheavy rainfall hitting Luna, Sierra, and western Dona Ana County \nbeginning around midnight.\n\nDrier air from Sonora and Arizona will enter southwestern New \nMexico by Friday afternoon, and PWATs are expected to trend down \nfrom 1.2\" to 1.0\". All the same, the 12Z HRRR has a few isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms in Hidalgo, Luna, and Grant Counties\npossible in the late afternoon hours, though I am skeptical if\nwestern New Mexico will see favorable conditions for thunderstorm\ndevelopment on Friday. The eastern reaches of our region will see\nmore favorable conditions for isolated-to-scattered thunderstorms\non Friday as moisture will be more plentiful there.\n\nSaturday and Sunday are both expected to be calm and warm as a\nridge hangs overhead. Then, subtropical moisture returns on late \nSunday night and will persist well into Tuesday. However, Monday \nis where the models begin to diverge in terms of how much moisture\nthe region will get, as well as if the region will see any \nimpacts from Polo; GFS is trending wetter, ECMWF is trending \ndrier. It is too soon to tell if Polo's remnants will impact the \nregion, but it is something we are watching closely.",
